WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.672 --> 00:00:11.758
I'm very grateful for being here
to share with you

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.885
that I live in a country, Uruguay,

00:00:13.927 --> 00:00:17.222
where about 98 percent of its electricity

00:00:17.222 --> 00:00:20.225
is obtained from a combination
of different renewable sources.

00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:24.688
A country whose inhabitants emit
30 times less greenhouse gases

00:00:24.729 --> 00:00:28.024
than the world average
for each kilowatt hour consumed.

00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:31.152
A country that produces
its electricity at a fixed cost,

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.322
regardless of wars
or other geopolitical events

00:00:34.364 --> 00:00:38.034
since it is almost independent
on energy commodities,

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:39.494
dependent just on its wind,

00:00:39.536 --> 00:00:42.080
its sun, its water, its biomass waste.

00:00:42.414 --> 00:00:47.043
A country that exports renewable energy
surpluses to its two giant neighbors,

00:00:47.043 --> 00:00:48.837
Argentina and Brazil.

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:52.883
When I tell people about Uruguay,
they usually say two things.

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:55.385
"I never heard about this.
How did you do it?"

00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:58.054
And, "Can this be replicated elsewhere?"

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.892
So today I want to tell you what happened
in Uruguay, how we did it.

00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.436
And yes, even if every country

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:07.147
would have to define
its own transition journey,

00:01:07.188 --> 00:01:10.650
the Uruguayan solution can definitely
be replicated elsewhere.

00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:15.947
Fifteen years ago, the Uruguayan
energy sector was going to a deep crisis.

00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:20.160
Economy was growing
in unprecedented rates

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.913
and poverty was decreasing,
which was so great, of course,

00:01:22.913 --> 00:01:25.624
but at the same time,

00:01:25.665 --> 00:01:29.628
energy demand was growing rapidly,
which was not so great.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:33.298
Uruguay has no proven reserve
for fossil fuels.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.385
We have already used our large rivers
to install hydropower plants

00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:40.347
and our two big neighbors
were having their own difficulties

00:01:40.388 --> 00:01:41.681
to supply internal demand,

00:01:41.723 --> 00:01:45.018
so it was not easy for them to help us.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.436
In dry years,

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:48.688
when you have to import
much more fossil fuels,

00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:51.733
we were forced to import electricity
from our neighbors

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:53.735
at extraordinary prices.

00:01:53.777 --> 00:01:57.113
Cost overruns could be
as high as one billion dollars.

00:01:57.155 --> 00:01:59.699
And for a small economy like Uruguay,

00:01:59.741 --> 00:02:02.118
this is two percent of its GDP.

00:02:02.786 --> 00:02:06.331
And worse still, we were beginning
to have blackouts.

00:02:06.665 --> 00:02:08.416
We were living the perfect storm.

00:02:09.084 --> 00:02:11.169
But crisis is also opportunity.

00:02:11.836 --> 00:02:14.839
For most of my life, I was a scientist,
a particle physicist,

00:02:14.881 --> 00:02:18.426
doing research on subjects far removed
from the everyday world.

00:02:18.843 --> 00:02:21.096
But in that particular national
energy context,

00:02:21.137 --> 00:02:22.973
I saw the need to reinvent myself,

00:02:23.014 --> 00:02:25.558
to get involved
in the search for solutions.

00:02:26.101 --> 00:02:29.729
I began to study the energy issue,
to organize seminars,

00:02:29.729 --> 00:02:31.523
to discuss with different experts,

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:34.275
and I realized how complex
the energy issue is,

00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:36.152
how many dimensions it has.

00:02:37.028 --> 00:02:39.614
Technological and economical
dimensions, of course,

00:02:39.614 --> 00:02:43.952
but also environmental,
social, geopolitical,

00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:46.621
cultural and even ethical dimensions.

00:02:46.955 --> 00:02:50.917
I started to write down my thoughts
and almost without realizing,

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:55.630
I ended up with a comprehensive,
holistic proposal

00:02:55.672 --> 00:02:58.675
focused on a just transition
to renewable energies

00:02:58.717 --> 00:03:00.385
with very ambitious goals.

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:01.886
And one day,

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:04.347
when I was in my office at university,

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:06.307
I received an unexpected call.

00:03:06.891 --> 00:03:11.021
My proposal had reached the president
and he was inviting me to implement it.

00:03:11.354 --> 00:03:12.605
Imagine my surprise.

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:14.733
He was inviting me
to be the political head

00:03:14.774 --> 00:03:16.776
of our national energy agency,

00:03:17.193 --> 00:03:19.988
what in the United States
would be a Secretary of Energy.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.616
I accepted, and we immediately began
to implement that policy.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:27.912
But before the result could be fully seen,
there was an election.

00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:31.583
Fortunately, the new
president, José Mujica,

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:34.919
was supportive of what
we had started to do

00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:36.963
and he asked me to remain in office.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.507
But he asked me one important point:

00:03:39.507 --> 00:03:43.303
to make this policy to be accepted
by all political parties.

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:48.391
Patiently, we negotiated with all parties
represented in Parliament

00:03:48.391 --> 00:03:51.519
and we achieved our goal after
they accepted some minor changes.

00:03:51.895 --> 00:03:53.730
Having a long-term policy

00:03:53.772 --> 00:03:56.441
backed by the entire
Uruguayan political system

00:03:56.483 --> 00:03:59.611
was crucial to make rapid progress.

00:03:59.903 --> 00:04:01.738
In just five years,

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.158
we went from a traditional
hydrothermal power mix

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.786
with up to 50 percent fossil share

00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:10.121
to an almost completely decarbonized one

00:04:10.121 --> 00:04:15.502
that already in 2017
was 98 percent renewable.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.421
What makes the
Uruguayan case unique

00:04:18.421 --> 00:04:21.341
is that almost half of that electricity

00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:24.511
is obtained from non-traditional
renewable sources.

00:04:24.511 --> 00:04:27.764
Wind, solar and sustainable biomass.

00:04:28.598 --> 00:04:32.894
Wind alone can produce up to 40 percent
of the total electricity

00:04:32.936 --> 00:04:34.604
consumed in the country in a year,

00:04:34.604 --> 00:04:38.525
a percentage comparable to the other
world wind champion, Denmark.

00:04:38.566 --> 00:04:43.071
But also 15 or even 20 percent
of our electricity

00:04:43.113 --> 00:04:46.116
is obtained with sustainable biomass.

00:04:46.658 --> 00:04:50.787
Uruguay is an agro-industrial country
with abundant organic waste

00:04:50.829 --> 00:04:56.042
that has a lot of energy content
like rice husks, bagasse,

00:04:56.084 --> 00:04:58.169
black liquors from pulp mills.

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:01.297
They have now ceased to be
an environmental liability

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:03.091
to become an energy asset.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.468
Of course, none of this was easy.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:06.719
We have to innovate.

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:10.640
We have to understand
that such a power system

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.226
requires the planning

00:05:13.268 --> 00:05:16.354
and operation quite different
than the traditional one.

00:05:17.188 --> 00:05:21.234
Our academics worked for years
to design a groundbreaking software

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:22.735
to handle energy dispatch,

00:05:22.777 --> 00:05:26.072
specifically designed to manage
intermittent sources

00:05:26.114 --> 00:05:29.617
such as wind and solar,
but also how to use water.

00:05:30.118 --> 00:05:33.413
This model is based on the probability

00:05:33.454 --> 00:05:36.082
of occurrence of different
weather scenarios

00:05:36.124 --> 00:05:39.127
based on both a century
of historical data

00:05:39.127 --> 00:05:40.920
and weather forecast.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.297
This allows us, for example,

00:05:42.338 --> 00:05:46.885
to have a prediction of the amount
of wind electricity and solar electricity

00:05:46.926 --> 00:05:49.971
that we're going to have in the grid
with a week in advance.

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:51.973
And this also allows us to know

00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:54.475
how and when we have to use
water from the dams.

00:05:55.143 --> 00:05:57.103
Thanks to this disruptive methodology,

00:05:57.145 --> 00:06:00.315
today in Uruguay,
intermittent sources are king.

00:06:01.024 --> 00:06:03.943
The total installed capacity
of wind plus solar

00:06:03.985 --> 00:06:07.322
is of the order of the total
country's peak demand.

00:06:07.322 --> 00:06:11.367
This means that when we have
enough wind and solar,

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:15.496
almost 100 percent of electricity
is just from these two sources alone,

00:06:15.538 --> 00:06:19.125
in addition to biomass-fired power plant.

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:22.629
And this may happen many times
during the course of a week.

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:26.883
And in those moments,
we don't use water from the dams.

00:06:26.883 --> 00:06:30.511
Hydropower plant only comes into play
when the sun goes down

00:06:30.553 --> 00:06:32.096
or when the wind falls.

00:06:33.056 --> 00:06:37.477
Our insurance policy,
when all else fails, are gas turbines,

00:06:37.518 --> 00:06:39.520
combined cycle and engine power plant.

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:40.730
Yes, of course,

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:45.068
flexible fossil-fired power
plants remain there,

00:06:45.068 --> 00:06:47.987
but we rely on them very little
over the course of a year,

00:06:48.029 --> 00:06:52.492
allowing non-renewable electricity
to be no more than two percent

00:06:52.533 --> 00:06:55.954
of the electricity in the grid
in a regular year,

00:06:55.954 --> 00:06:59.999
and sometimes six or seven percent
in a very dry one.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:02.001
And get this.

00:07:02.001 --> 00:07:05.755
We achieve this goal without
any single battery storage,

00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:10.802
water pumping or any modern technology
for electricity storage.

00:07:11.302 --> 00:07:14.931
Uruguay has shown
that the power mix can work

00:07:14.931 --> 00:07:18.309
almost exclusively thanks
to the complementarity

00:07:18.351 --> 00:07:20.186
of different renewable sources

00:07:20.186 --> 00:07:23.314
independently of their
individual intermittency.

00:07:24.023 --> 00:07:27.318
And this is perhaps the time
for you to ask the question,

00:07:27.318 --> 00:07:28.945
“And what happened to the economy?

00:07:28.945 --> 00:07:30.613
How much did all this cost?”

00:07:31.322 --> 00:07:33.283
And the answer is even more radical.

00:07:33.324 --> 00:07:36.911
The cost was negative,
if I may put it this way.

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:40.290
In fact, we get an impressive
positive impact to our economy.

00:07:40.748 --> 00:07:41.916
To begin with,

00:07:41.958 --> 00:07:45.211
the total cost to produce
electricity all over a year

00:07:45.211 --> 00:07:47.588
was reduced by almost half.

00:07:48.172 --> 00:07:51.301
No surprise, renewable energies
are the cheapest option today.

00:07:52.176 --> 00:07:55.972
We went from about 1.1 billion dollar cost

00:07:55.972 --> 00:07:59.017
to only 600 million dollars today.

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:04.147
And this impressive
500 million dollars every year

00:08:04.188 --> 00:08:06.107
for a country like Uruguay is huge.

00:08:06.107 --> 00:08:08.568
It's one percent of its GDP.

00:08:08.568 --> 00:08:12.238
Just for comparison,
one percent of United States GDP,

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:16.284
it's about 250 billion dollars annually.

00:08:16.701 --> 00:08:21.039
Moreover, those tremendous cost overruns
that we had in dry years,

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:22.999
well, they virtually disappeared.

00:08:23.416 --> 00:08:27.420
We went from cost overruns
of about one billion dollars,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.591
to only 100 to perhaps 200 million a year

00:08:31.591 --> 00:08:33.134
in very dry ones.

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:34.969
Surprisingly enough

00:08:34.969 --> 00:08:39.098
now, though, to have much more
natural sources in the grid,

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.017
because of their complementarity,

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:43.936
we are much less dependent
on weather variability.

00:08:44.437 --> 00:08:48.191
Of course, achieving this tremendous
cost reduction was not easy either.

00:08:48.232 --> 00:08:50.526
We have to innovate also on that.

00:08:50.526 --> 00:08:52.195
We have to understand

00:08:52.236 --> 00:08:55.281
that renewable energies
require a business model

00:08:55.323 --> 00:08:58.368
quite different from the traditional one

00:08:58.409 --> 00:09:00.661
of the power sectors all over the world.

00:09:01.245 --> 00:09:04.999
We have to create a new market model.

00:09:05.541 --> 00:09:08.795
Our present business model
is based on long-term contracts

00:09:08.836 --> 00:09:10.963
derived from auction processes

00:09:10.963 --> 00:09:15.760
in which the percentage
of this source is predetermined

00:09:15.802 --> 00:09:17.220
using an optimization model

00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:20.348
that defines which is the best
technical complementarity

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:21.557
between sources

00:09:21.599 --> 00:09:24.519
in order to minimize
the overall cost of the system.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.189
Thanks to this, nowadays,

00:09:28.231 --> 00:09:31.692
almost 100 percent
of our electricity generation

00:09:31.692 --> 00:09:32.860
is under contract,

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:35.613
leaving almost no space
for the spot market

00:09:35.655 --> 00:09:39.075
and also no space for uncertainties.

00:09:39.617 --> 00:09:42.620
The comprehensive model
we had defined and put in place

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:45.748
not only allows us to strongly
reduce the overall costs,

00:09:45.748 --> 00:09:48.042
but also to stabilize it over time.

00:09:48.751 --> 00:09:53.464
The cost to produce electricity
in Uruguay today is stabilized.

00:09:53.506 --> 00:09:57.844
Think it is almost independent
of the fluctuation of the prices

00:09:57.885 --> 00:09:59.554
of energy commodities.

00:09:59.971 --> 00:10:02.348
Thanks to this, for example,

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:05.059
the Uruguayan electric mix
was barely affected

00:10:05.059 --> 00:10:06.436
by the tragic war in Europe.

00:10:06.477 --> 00:10:09.522
We had no supply
nor affordability difficulties.

00:10:10.022 --> 00:10:13.234
But the positive economic impact
of this transformation

00:10:13.276 --> 00:10:15.778
went far beyond just the electric sector.

00:10:16.362 --> 00:10:19.907
We achieved a tremendous
impact to our economy.

00:10:19.949 --> 00:10:22.785
We received six billion
dollars in investment.

00:10:22.785 --> 00:10:25.037
This is 12 percent of our GDP.

00:10:25.079 --> 00:10:27.457
Imagine the impact to our economy.

00:10:27.915 --> 00:10:31.544
New knowledge, new industrial,
entrepreneurial capabilities,

00:10:31.544 --> 00:10:34.005
50,000 jobs were created.

00:10:34.046 --> 00:10:35.756
This seems small, perhaps for you,

00:10:35.756 --> 00:10:39.760
but for a country with only
3.4 million inhabitants,

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.513
it represents three percent
of its labor force.

00:10:42.847 --> 00:10:45.183
Once again, if we were made
to make a comparison,

00:10:45.224 --> 00:10:47.435
three percent of the United
States labor force

00:10:47.435 --> 00:10:50.605
is several million new jobs created.

00:10:51.147 --> 00:10:54.692
So I told you a little about what happened
in Uruguay and how we did it.

00:10:54.734 --> 00:10:57.361
But you may be thinking,
“OK, but that’s Uruguay,

00:10:57.361 --> 00:10:58.571
fine, but that's Uruguay.

00:10:58.613 --> 00:10:59.822
It's a small country.

00:10:59.822 --> 00:11:01.115
They have a unique culture.

00:11:01.157 --> 00:11:02.450
They have hydro.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.911
Can this be reproduced elsewhere?”

00:11:04.952 --> 00:11:07.413
So I want to be very emphatic on this.

00:11:07.955 --> 00:11:12.460
Although each country will have to define
its own transition process,

00:11:12.502 --> 00:11:17.507
the vast majority of countries can make
a process similar to the Uruguayan one.

00:11:17.548 --> 00:11:20.927
(Applause)

00:11:20.927 --> 00:11:25.097
You don't necessarily need a lot of hydro
backup capacity, but for sure,

00:11:25.097 --> 00:11:28.142
you need to put much more
flexibility to your system

00:11:28.142 --> 00:11:30.228
with a new dispatch model,

00:11:30.228 --> 00:11:32.230
a new market model.

00:11:32.688 --> 00:11:38.319
But if you have people and companies
doing research and developing AI

00:11:38.361 --> 00:11:41.781
and artificial intelligence and rockets,

00:11:41.822 --> 00:11:45.493
developing a new market model,
a new energy management,

00:11:45.493 --> 00:11:47.036
shouldn't be that hard.

00:11:47.537 --> 00:11:48.704
By doing this,

00:11:48.746 --> 00:11:52.542
we will probably not get the fossil
share down to two percent,

00:11:52.583 --> 00:11:55.211
but for sure it will be below 15
or even 20 percent

00:11:55.253 --> 00:11:57.713
and in many countries, 
much less than that.

00:11:58.548 --> 00:12:00.758
And you know what,
for the last couple of years

00:12:00.758 --> 00:12:02.176
after leaving the government,

00:12:02.218 --> 00:12:04.762
I've been working in a number of countries

00:12:04.762 --> 00:12:07.598
from Dominican Republic to Chile

00:12:07.598 --> 00:12:10.518
and many other countries
in Latin America, but also in Europe,

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.727
and other regions.

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:13.062
And the good news is

00:12:13.062 --> 00:12:19.026
that the Uruguayan solution can work
in different national and energy context.

00:12:19.986 --> 00:12:22.738
But for that, there is one
special ingredient.

00:12:23.197 --> 00:12:25.825
You need to have a strong leadership

00:12:25.825 --> 00:12:28.244
and a strong political will
to move forward.

00:12:28.286 --> 00:12:29.620
And for that,

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:33.416
it's mostly recommended to build
a broad political agreement

00:12:33.457 --> 00:12:38.004
that transcends the administration
in office in order to be sure

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:40.464
that the transition will continue in time.

00:12:41.215 --> 00:12:44.427
What is then the most important
message for you today?

00:12:44.719 --> 00:12:46.095
It is very simple.

00:12:46.679 --> 00:12:51.309
That renewables are no longer
just a solution for the climate crisis.

00:12:51.767 --> 00:12:57.481
Renewables allow us to build strong,
reliable, robust power systems.

00:12:57.523 --> 00:13:01.819
They allow us to sharply reduce
and stabilize the electricity costs,

00:13:01.861 --> 00:13:06.240
while at the same time
having an important energy boost

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.369
to our economies and job creation.

00:13:12.204 --> 00:13:15.207
Lessons learned from the Uruguayan case

00:13:15.207 --> 00:13:18.711
are that renewable energies
are ripe to provide

00:13:18.753 --> 00:13:22.506
not just a climate solution
on a global scale,

00:13:22.548 --> 00:13:26.886
but also substantial socio-economic
benefits at a local level.

00:13:26.886 --> 00:13:30.431
And this can and should happen now.

00:13:30.473 --> 00:13:33.267
There's no reason to wait until 2040,

00:13:33.309 --> 00:13:34.769
not even 2030.

00:13:35.144 --> 00:13:36.312
Now.

00:13:36.354 --> 00:13:37.480
Thank you.

00:13:37.521 --> 00:13:42.693
(Applause and cheers)

